      "body": "## Motivation\n\nFixes #41748.\n\n`/tokenize` (and `/v1/tokenize`) reported `max_model_len` from the\ntokenizer's `model_max_length`, not from the limit the server actually\nenforces. For Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B the tokenizer says 131072 while a server\nrunning with `context_len` 40960 rejects any prompt at or above 40960\nwith a 400, so a client budgeting prompts against `/tokenize` was told\n131072 tokens fit and then failed for prompts between 40961 and 131072\ntokens.\n\nThis also disagreed with `/v1/models`, which already reports\n`model_config.context_len` as `max_model_len`.\n\n## Modifications\n\n- `python/sglang/srt/entrypoints/openai/serving_tokenize.py`:\n  `OpenAIServingTokenize._handle_non_streaming_request` now reports\n  `model_config.context_len` as `max_model_len`. This covers both the\n  `/v1/tokenize` and `/tokenize` routes, which share this handler. The\n  old fallback for absurd (out-of-int64-range) tokenizer values is gone\n  with the tokenizer-derived value itself; tokenization behavior is\n  unchanged, only the reported limit.\n- `test/registered/unit/entrypoints/openai/test_serving_tokenize.py`:\n  added `test_max_model_len_is_server_context_len`, covering a text\n  prompt, a batched prompt, and chat messages with a tokenizer whose\n  `model_max_length` (131072) exceeds the server's `context_len`\n  (4096 in the mock). A prior PR for this issue (#41785) was closed by\n  automation for inactivity, not on merit; this re-applies its approach\n  on current main.\n\n## Tests\n\n- The new regression test lives in the existing CPU unit test file\n  `test/registered/unit/entrypoints/openai/test_serving_tokenize.py`.\n  It could not be executed in this environment (no torch/sglang\n  install here), so it needs CI.\n- Verified without a GPU here via a stub harness that executes the real\n  `_handle_non_streaming_request` with a fake tokenizer\n  (`model_max_length=131072`) and a fake model config\n  (`context_len=4096`): text and batched prompts return\n  `max_model_len=4096` with unchanged token ids/counts. The tokenizer\n  behavior the test relies on (setting `model_max_length`, `<bos>`\n  post-processing) was confirmed against real `transformers`/\n  `tokenizers`.\n- `ruff check --select F401,F821,F823,UP037`, `ruff format --check`,\n  `python3 -m py_compile`, and `git diff --check` all pass on both\n  touched files. No existing test contradicts the change: the only\n  other `max_model_len` assertion in the tree covers `/v1/models`,\n  which is untouched.\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:x: [Run #40881447131](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/40881447131)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #40881447083](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/40881447083)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\nLatest PR Test (AMD ROCm 10): <!-- slot:pr-test-amd:start -->:x: [Run #40881446666](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/40881446666)<!-- slot:pr-test-amd:end -->\n<!-- pr-states:end -->",
